euvre\n# Feature description: Normalized value of Vehicle_Manoeuvre using min-max scaling across all observations.\n# Usefulness: This normalizes the type of vehicle manoeuvre, pointing to the context of the accident. Certain manoeuvres (e.g., sharp turns) might exhibit different likelihoods across driver sexes.\ndf['Relative_Manoeuvre'] = (df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'] - df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'].min()) / (df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'].max() - df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'].min())\nend\n\n---\n\nColumns Dropped for Redundancy/Low Information:\n\n1. Drop Hit_Object_in_Carriageway and Hit_Object_off_Carriageway  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Hit_Object_in_Carriageway is dropped:\n# It has a narrow range (0–12) with most values concentrated at 0.0 (Median = Q3).\n# This feature is highly sparse and unlikely to contribute significantly to modeling the sex of the driver.\n\n# Explanation why the column Hit_Object_off_Carriageway is dropped:\n# Similarly, this column shows little variation with concentration around 0.0. It isn't differentiating enough to correlate strongly with the target variable.\ndf.drop(columns=['Hit_Object_in_Carriageway', 'Hit_Object_off_Carriageway'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n2. Drop Casualty_IMD_Decile  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_IMD_Decile is dropped:\n# Represents the decile of deprivation index for the casualty but remains weakly relevant to the sex of the driver in scenarios where the casualty isn't directly tied to the driver.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_IMD_Decile'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nThese dropped columns allow us to focus on meaningful signals, while the added features aim at capturing relationships aligned with the task.\n\n--- \nThese steps aim to increase downstream model performance by deriving informative cross-features, grouping context-based insights, and normalizing key behavioral attributes. They also improve readability and reduce noise!"]###,
