rian_Location is dropped  \n# Has sparse values concentrated at 0 (Median = Q3 = 0). Its limited variability likely makes it insignificant for predicting driver sex.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Pedestrian_Location'], inplace=True)\n```end  \n\nDrop `Pedestrian_Movement`  \n```python\n# Explanation why the column Pedestrian_Movement is dropped  \n# Similar to Pedestrian_Location, this column is sparse with values concentrated at 0 and is unlikely to provide significant predictive power for driver sex.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Pedestrian_Movement'], inplace=True)\n```end  \n\nDrop `2nd_Road_Number`  \n```python\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped  \n# This column has limited relevance as accidents often occur on primary roads, and its contribution to the prediction task is negligible due to sparsity.  \ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\n```end  \n\nThese steps focus on intuitive real-world correlations, extracted normalized features, and simplifying the dataset to improve the downstream model performance without redundancy or noise.", "### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nStep 1: **Create Age Band and Engine Capacity Interaction**\n```python\n# Feature name: Age_Band_Engine_Capacity_Interaction\n# Feature description: Multiply Age_Band_of_Driver and Engine_Capacity_(CC) to capture how driver age group and vehicle engine power interact.\n# Usefulness: Drivers in different age groups may exhibit different preferences for engine power, which could correlate with the sex of the driver. For instance, younger drivers may seek higher engine capacities for speed, whereas older drivers may select more modest ones.\ndf['Age_Band_Engine_Capacity_Interaction'] = df['Age_Band_of_Driver'] * df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)']\n```end\n\nStep 2: **Log Transformation of Engine Capacity**\n```python\n# Feature name: Log_Engine_Capacity\n# Feature description: Apply the natural logarithm to Engine_Capacity_(CC) to reduce the effect of extreme values.\n# Usefulness: Engine capacities range widely (48 to 18,000), and log transformation can stabilize variance and help the model learn better by emphasizing proportional changes rather than sheer magnitudes.\ndf['Log_Engine_Capacity'] = np.log1p(df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'])\n```end\n\nStep 3: **Create Urban/Rural Accident Severity Interaction**\n```python\n# Feature name: Urban_Rural_Severity_Interaction\n# Feature description: Multiply Urban_or_Rural_Area and Accident_Severity_Factor to capture the interaction between accident location type and severity.\n# Usefulness: Accident severity might differ based on urban/rural settings, and this interaction could provide insights into gender-related differences in the driver’s role in different environments.\ndf['Urban_Rural_Severity_Interaction'] = df['Urban_or_Rural_Area'] * df['Accident_Severity_Factor']\n```end\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns for Redundancy or Low Predictive Power\n\nDrop `Pedestrian_Location`  \n```python\n# Explanation why the column Pedestrian_Location is dropped\n# The value in this column is concentrated around 0.0 (with little variability or relevance to the driver's sex) and contributes little to predicting the target variable.\ndf.drop(columns=['Pedestrian_Location'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\nDrop `2nd_Road_Number`  \n```python\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped\n# The column has high sparsity (most values are 0.0) and does not directly provide meaningful information about the driver's sex.\ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\nDrop `Casualty_IMD_Decile`  \n```python\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_IMD_Decile is dropped\n# This column represents the deprivation index of the casualty area, which might have weak or no direct correlation with the sex of the driver.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_IMD_Decile'], inplace=True)\n```end\n\n---\n\nThese steps derive meaningful interactions from existing features, reduce the impact of extreme values with