gnal while increasing redundancy.\ndf.drop(columns=['Vehicle_Reference_df_res'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nDrop Longitude and Latitude  \npython\n# Explanation why Longitude and Latitude are dropped\n# Longitude and Latitude can be replaced with spatial binning features, allowing the model to generalize better with fewer dimensions while retaining geographical information.\ndf.drop(columns=['Longitude', 'Latitude'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nDrop Hit_Object_in_Carriageway and Hit_Object_off_Carriageway  \npython\n# Explanation why Hit_Object_in_Carriageway and Hit_Object_off_Carriageway are dropped\n# Both columns show extremely sparse distributions (low variability concentrated around 0). They are unlikely to provide significant predictive power for the task.\ndf.drop(columns=['Hit_Object_in_Carriageway', 'Hit_Object_off_Carriageway'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\nThese steps add intuitive features rooted in real-world behavior patterns and reduce noise from uninformative columns, improving the model's focus on predictive signals relevant to the driver’s sex.", "### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nStep 1: Add Driver Age Category  \npython\n# Feature name: Driver_Age_Category\n# Feature description: Categorize Age_of_Driver into age groups: young (<25), middle-aged (25-65), and senior (>65).\n# Usefulness: Age categories capture differences in driving behavior and preferences, which could correlate with driver sex. These age groups align with common demographic risk profiles.\ndf['Driver_Age_Category'] = pd.cut(df['Age_of_Driver'], bins=[0, 25, 65, 100], labels=['young', 'middle-aged', 'senior'])\nend\n\nStep 2: Compute Accident Density Factor  \npython\n# Feature name: Accident_Density_Factor\n# Feature description: Multiply Urban_or_Rural_Area and Accident_Severity_Factor to account for accident density in different regions.\n# Usefulness: Urban and rural areas may significantly differ in accident patterns and severity. This feature can highlight environment-based tendencies that may correlate with driver sex.\ndf['Accident_Density_Factor'] = df['Urban_or_Rural_Area'] * df['Accident_Severity_Factor']\nend\n\nStep 3: Ratio of Engine Capacity to Vehicle Age  \npython\n# Feature name: Engine_Capacity_to_Vehicle_Age_Ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of Engine_Capacity_(CC) to Age_of_Vehicle (adjusted to avoid division by 0).\n# Usefulness: This captures how powerful a vehicle is compared to its age. Drivers with preferences for newer, high-capacity cars may reflect gender tendencies in vehicle choice, making this feature useful.\ndf['Engine_Capacity_to_Vehicle_Age_Ratio'] = df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'] / (df['Age_of_Vehicle'] + 1)\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns for Redundancy or Low Predictive Power\n\nDrop Pedestrian_Location  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Pedestrian_Location is dropped\n# The column is sparse, with most values concentrated at 0, indicating low variability and limited correlation with predicting the driver's sex.\ndf.drop(columns=['Pedestrian_Location'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nDrop 2nd_Road_Number  \npython\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped\n# This column has high sparsity, with values concentrated at 0, and offers low predictive signal for the target variable.\ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nDrop Police_Force  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Police_Force is dropped\n# Represents categorical values for different police jurisdictions, which are unlikely to provide meaningful correlation with driver gender.\ndf.drop(columns=['Police_Force'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n--- \n\nThese steps introduce useful and intuitive features rooted in real-world patterns while reducing noise from irrelevant columns, aiming to improve downstream model performance."), with improvements -
