###["### Three Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nStep 1: Add interaction features using engine capacity and driver's age.\npython\n# Feature name: Engine_Capacity_Age_Product\n# Feature description: Product of Engine_Capacity_(CC) and Age_of_Driver.\n# Usefulness: This captures the correlation between the type of vehicle (indicated by engine capacity) and the driver's age. Younger drivers might prefer vehicles with high engine capacity for performance, while older drivers might prefer moderate engine capacities. This also aligns with real-world driving behavior patterns influenced by age and vehicle type.\ndf['Engine_Capacity_Age_Product'] = df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'] * df['Age_of_Driver']\nend\n\nStep 2: Create location-based binning features.\npython\n# Feature name: Latitude_Longitude_Bin\n# Feature description: Bin the Latitude and Longitude coordinates into regions to group accidents based on geographical patterns.\n# Usefulness: Grouping spatial data into bins can capture regional driving habits or trends. For example, rural areas might have different accident patterns compared to urban areas, which can indirectly correlate with driver demographics (e.g., gender differences in rural vs. urban driving habits).\ndf['Latitude_Bin'] = pd.cut(df['Latitude'], bins=5, labels=False)\ndf['Longitude_Bin'] = pd.cut(df['Longitude'], bins=5, labels=False)\nend\n\nStep 3: Extract the accident severity based on the number of vehicles involved and casualties.\npython\n# Feature name: Accident_Severity_Score\n# Feature description: Compute an aggregated score by combining Number_of_Vehicles and Number_of_Casualties involved in the accident.\n# Usefulness: This creates a proxy for the severity of an accident. Severe accidents may correlate with specific demographic trends of drivers (e.g., younger or more aggressive drivers being involved in more severe accidents).\ndf['Accident_Severity_Score'] = (df['Number_of_Vehicles'] + df['Number_of_Casualties']) / 2\nend\n\n### Dropping Unnecessary Columns\n\nSome columns may not provide useful predictive information for this task or may overlap entirely with new features.\npython\n# Explanation why the column Vehicle_Reference_df_res is dropped\n# Vehicle_Reference_df_res is redundant due to the inclusion of Vehicle_Reference_df, which overlaps functionally.\ndf.drop(columns=['Vehicle_Reference_df_res'], inplace=True)\n\n# Explanation why the column Longitude is dropped\n# Longitude will be replaced by its binned version Longitude_Bin, which provides more generalizable location-based insights.\ndf.drop(columns=['Longitude'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Possible Next Three Steps:\n\nStep 1: Create Age Bracket Interaction with Sex of Casualty\npython\n# Feature name: Age_Band_of_Driver_and_Sex_of_Casualty\n# Feature description: Interaction between the Age_Band_of_Driver and Sex_of_Casualty as categorical attributes.\n# Usefulness: This creates a cross feature, capturing possible correlations between the age band of the driver and the sex of the casualty. For example, drivers of certain age groups may tend to have different interactions in accidents with male or female casualties, revealing hidden patterns relevant to the driver's sex.\ndf['Age_Band_of_Driver_and_Sex_of_Casualty'] = df['Age_Band_of_Driver'].astype(str) + _ + df['Sex_of_Casualty'].astype(str)\nend\n\nStep 2: Group-based Mean Engine Capacity by Vehicle Type\npython\n# Feature name: Mean_Engine_Capacity_by_Vehicle_Type\n# Feature description: The average engine capacity (Engine_Capacity_(CC)) grouped by Vehicle_Type.\n# Usefulness: This gives insight into the typical engine capacity for different vehicle types, which could influence driving habits or preferences that are indirectly linked to the sex of the driver.\ndf['Mean_Engine_Capacity_by_Vehicle_Type'] = df.groupby('Vehicle_Type')['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'].transform('mean')\nend\n\nStep 3: Extract Relative Vehicle Manoeuvre Direction\npython\n# Feature name: Relative_Manoeuvre\n# Feature description: Normalized value of Vehicle_Manoeuvre using min-max scaling across all observations.\n# Usefulness: This normalizes the type of vehicle manoeuvre, pointing to the context of the accident. Certain manoeuvres (e.g., sharp turns) might exhibit different likelihoods across driver sexes.\ndf['Relative_Manoeuvre'] = (df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'] - df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'].min()) / (df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'].max() - df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'].min())\nend\n\n---\n\nColumns Dropped for Redundancy/Low Information:\n\n1. Drop Hit_Object_in_Carriageway and Hit_Object_off_Carriageway  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Hit_Object_in_Carriageway is dropped:\n# It has a narrow range (0–12) with most values concentrated at 0.0 (Median = Q3).\n# This feature is highly sparse and unlikely to contribute significantly to modeling the sex of the driver.\n\n# Explanation why the column Hit_Object_off_Carriageway is dropped:\n# Similarly, this column shows little variation with concentration around 0.0. It isn't differentiating enough to correlate strongly with the target variable.\ndf.drop(columns=['Hit_Object_in_Carriageway', 'Hit_Object_off_Carriageway'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n2. Drop Casualty_IMD_Decile  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_IMD_Decile is dropped:\n# Represents the decile of deprivation index for the casualty but remains weakly relevant to the sex of the driver in scenarios where the casualty isn't directly tied to the driver.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_IMD_Decile'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nThese dropped columns allow us to focus on meaningful signals, while the added features aim at capturing relationships aligned with the task.\n\n--- \nThese steps aim to increase downstream model performance by deriving informative cross-features, grouping context-based insights, and normalizing key behavioral attributes. They also improve readability and reduce noise!", "### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nStep 1: Create Normalized Accident Severity Factor  \npython\n# Feature name: Normalized_Accident_Severity  \n# Feature description: Normalize the Accident_Severity_Factor using min-max scaling.  \n# Usefulness: Helps compare the severity of accidents on a consistent scale which may correlate with driver behaviors (e.g., risk aversion versus risk-taking) and indirectly their gender.  \ndf['Normalized_Accident_Severity'] = (df['Accident_Severity_Factor'] - df['Accident_Severity_Factor'].min()) / (df['Accident_Severity_Factor'].max() - df['Accident_Severity_Factor'].min())\nend  \n\nStep 2: Interaction Between Driver Age Band and Propulsion Code  \npython\n# Feature name: Driver_Age_Band_Propulsion_Interaction  \n# Feature description: Multiply Age_Band_of_Driver and Propulsion_Code to capture interactions between driver age range and vehicle propulsion type.  \n# Usefulness: Younger drivers might favor electric or hybrid propulsion, while older drivers may prefer conventional engines. Capturing this interaction could reveal correlations with gender dynamics in vehicle choice.  \ndf['Driver_Age_Band_Propulsion_Interaction'] = df['Age_Band_of_Driver'] * df['Propulsion_Code']\nend  \n\nStep 3: Urban or Rural Accident Proportion  \npython\n# Feature name: Urban_Rural_Accident_Proportion  \n# Feature description: Compute proportion of accidents occurring in urban versus rural areas (normalized by Urban_or_Rural_Area).  \n# Usefulness: Driving behaviors may vary between urban and rural locations and can influence accident statistics, potentially reflecting gender-based driving preferences or tendencies.  \ndf['Urban_Rural_Accident_Proportion'] = df['Urban_or_Rural_Area'] / df['Urban_or_Rural_Area'].sum()\nend  \n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns for Redundancy or Low Information  \n\nDrop Pedestrian_Location  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Pedestrian_Location is dropped  \n# Has sparse values concentrated at 0 (Median = Q3 = 0). Its limited variability likely makes it insignificant for predicting driver sex.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Pedestrian_Location'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nDrop Pedestrian_Movement  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Pedestrian_Movement is dropped  \n# Similar to Pedestrian_Location, this column is sparse with values concentrated at 0 and is unlikely to provide significant predictive power for driver sex.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Pedestrian_Movement'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nDrop 2nd_Road_Number  \npython\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped  \n# This column has limited relevance as accidents often occur on primary roads, and its contribution to the prediction task is negligible due to sparsity.  \ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nThese steps focus on intuitive real-world correlations, extracted normalized features, and simplifying the dataset to improve the downstream model performance without redundancy or noise.", "### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nStep 1: Create Age Band and Engine Capacity Interaction\npython\n# Feature name: Age_Band_Engine_Capacity_Interaction\n# Feature description: Multiply Age_Band_of_Driver and Engine_Capacity_(CC) to capture how driver age group and vehicle engine power interact.\n# Usefulness: Drivers in different age groups may exhibit different preferences for engine power, which could correlate with the sex of the driver. For instance, younger drivers may seek higher engine capacities for speed, whereas older drivers may select more modest ones.\ndf['Age_Band_Engine_Capacity_Interaction'] = df['Age_Band_of_Driver'] * df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)']\nend\n\nStep 2: Log Transformation of Engine Capacity\npython\n# Feature name: Log_Engine_Capacity\n# Feature description: Apply the natural logarithm to Engine_Capacity_(CC) to reduce the effect of extreme values.\n# Usefulness: Engine capacities range widely (48 to 18,000), and log transformation can stabilize variance and help the model learn better by emphasizing proportional changes rather than sheer magnitudes.\ndf['Log_Engine_Capacity'] = np.log1p(df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'])\nend\n\nStep 3: Create Urban/Rural Accident Severity Interaction\npython\n# Feature name: Urban_Rural_Severity_Interaction\n# Feature description: Multiply Urban_or_Rural_Area and Accident_Severity_Factor to capture the interaction between accident location type and severity.\n# Usefulness: Accident severity might differ based on urban/rural settings, and this interaction could provide insights into gender-related differences in the driver’s role in different environments.\ndf['Urban_Rural_Severity_Interaction'] = df['Urban_or_Rural_Area'] * df['Accident_Severity_Factor']\nend\n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns for Redundancy or Low Predictive Power\n\nDrop Pedestrian_Location  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Pedestrian_Location is dropped\n# The value in this column is concentrated around 0.0 (with little variability or relevance to the driver's sex) and contributes little to predicting the target variable.\ndf.drop(columns=['Pedestrian_Location'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nDrop 2nd_Road_Number  \npython\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped\n# The column has high sparsity (most values are 0.0) and does not directly provide meaningful information about the driver's sex.\ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nDrop Casualty_IMD_Decile  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_IMD_Decile is dropped\n# This column represents the deprivation index of the casualty area, which might have weak or no direct correlation with the sex of the driver.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_IMD_Decile'], inplace=True)\nend\n\n---\n\nThese steps derive meaningful interactions from existing features, reduce the impact of extreme values with log transformation, and eliminate redundant or irrelevant features to improve the predictive performance of the downstream model.", "### Three Possible Next Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nStep 1: Extract Ratio of Engine Capacity to Vehicle Age  \npython\n# Feature name: Engine_Capacity_to_Vehicle_Age_Ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of Engine_Capacity_(CC) to Age_of_Vehicle.\n# Usefulness: This feature captures how powerful the vehicle is relative to its age. Drivers seeking high-performance vehicles may prefer newer cars with higher engine capacities. This ratio may indirectly correlate with the driver's sex by reflecting preferences and affordability trends.\ndf['Engine_Capacity_to_Vehicle_Age_Ratio'] = df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'] / (df['Age_of_Vehicle'] + 1)\nend\n\nStep 2: Create Interaction Between Driver's Age and Casualty's Age  \npython\n# Feature name: Age_of_Driver_and_Casualty_Interaction\n# Feature description: Multiply Age_of_Driver and Age_of_Casualty.\n# Usefulness: Interactions between the driver's age and the casualty's age might reveal patterns about demographic behaviors during accidents. For instance, younger drivers might tend to be involved in certain types of accidents compared to older drivers, which might correlate with genders indirectly.\ndf['Age_of_Driver_and_Casualty_Interaction'] = df['Age_of_Driver'] * df['Age_of_Casualty']\nend\n\nStep 3: Group-Based Mean of Vehicle Complexity by Vehicle Type  \npython\n# Feature name: Mean_Vehicle_Complexity_by_Vehicle_Type\n# Feature description: Compute the mean Vehicle_Complexity_Score grouped by Vehicle_Type.\n# Usefulness: Certain vehicle types might have a higher complexity score on average, which could translate to driver preferences for specific vehicle categories. This grouped mean provides additional insight into correlations between vehicle choice and driver behavior, which may be influenced by gender.\ndf['Mean_Vehicle_Complexity_by_Vehicle_Type'] = df.groupby('Vehicle_Type')['Vehicle_Complexity_Score'].transform('mean')\nend\n\n### Dropping Columns for Redundancy or Low Predictive Power\n\nDrop Latitude and Longitude After Binning  \npython\n# Explanation why the columns Latitude and Longitude are dropped\n# Latitude and Longitude have been replaced by Latitude_Bin and Longitude_Bin, respectively, which provide more generalized and useful regional cluster information for predictive modeling.\ndf.drop(columns=['Latitude', 'Longitude'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nDrop Casualty_Reference  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Casualty_Reference is dropped\n# Casualty_Reference acts as an identifier rather than providing meaningful information for predicting the driver's sex. It doesn’t contribute to understanding the dataset features.\ndf.drop(columns=['Casualty_Reference'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nDrop 1st_Road_Number  \npython\n# Explanation why the column 1st_Road_Number is dropped\n# This column has a wide range of values but holds little relevance to the task of predicting the driver's sex. Its variability isn’t tied to meaningful patterns for modeling.\ndf.drop(columns=['1st_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend\n\nThese steps aim to introduce meaningful feature interactions and group-based summaries, while simplifying the dataset by removing redundant or irrelevant columns for effective model training.", "### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nStep 1: Compute Engine Capacity Per Vehicle Manoeuvre  \npython\n# Feature name: Engine_Capacity_Per_Manoeuvre\n# Feature description: Engine capacity divided by Vehicle_Manoeuvre.\n# Usefulness: Captures how the engine capacity influences the type of manoeuvre during an accident. Drivers with specific sexes might prefer vehicles with particular performance characteristics, and this interaction could reveal new patterns.\ndf['Engine_Capacity_Per_Manoeuvre'] = df['Engine_Capacity_(CC)'] / (df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'] + 1)  # Adding 1 to avoid division by zero.\nend\n\nStep 2: Create Casualty Gender Ratio per Case  \npython\n# Feature name: Casualty_Gender_Ratio\n# Feature description: Ratio of male casualties (Sex_of_Casualty = 1) to total casualties in an accident.\n# Usefulness: Incorporates the gender distribution of casualties, which might relate to driving behavior and patterns by the driver's gender.\ndf['Casualty_Gender_Ratio'] = df.groupby('Age_of_Driver')['Sex_of_Casualty'].transform(lambda x: (x == 1).sum() / len(x))\nend\n\nStep 3: Classify Age into Risk Groups  \npython\n# Feature name: Age_Risk_Group\n# Feature description: Classify Age_of_Driver into risk bands [low, medium, high] based on age brackets (e.g., <25, 25-65, >65).\n# Usefulness: Drivers in different age groups display distinct driving behaviors and accident profiles, which could correlate with their gender.\ndf['Age_Risk_Group'] = pd.cut(df['Age_of_Driver'], bins=[0, 25, 65, 100], labels=['low', 'medium', 'high'])\nend\n\n---\n\n### Columns Dropped for Redundancy or Low Predictive Power  \n\nDrop 2nd_Road_Number  \npython\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped\n# This column is sparse, with most values concentrated at 0.0, representing little variability or relevance to predicting the driver's sex.\ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nDrop Vehicle_Location-Restricted_Lane  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Vehicle_Location-Restricted_Lane is dropped\n# The column contains mostly 0 values, indicating low signal and no strong correlation with the prediction of the driver's sex.\ndf.drop(columns=['Vehicle_Location-Restricted_Lane'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nDrop Police_Force  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Police_Force is dropped\n# This column represents categorical values for different police forces, which likely correlate weakly with driver gender and provide no meaningful geographical or demographic insight beyond existing location features.\ndf.drop(columns=['Police_Force'], inplace=True)\nend", "### Possible Next Three Steps for Feature Engineering\n\nStep 1: Compute Accident Rate per Vehicle Manoeuvre  \npython\n# Feature name: Accident_Rate_Per_Manoeuvre  \n# Feature description: Ratio of Accident_Severity_Factor to Vehicle_Manoeuvre.  \n# Usefulness: Certain manoeuvres might lead to more severe accidents. Capturing the relative rate of accidents per manoeuvre could provide insights into behavioral differences between drivers of different sexes.\ndf['Accident_Rate_Per_Manoeuvre'] = df['Accident_Severity_Factor'] / (df['Vehicle_Manoeuvre'] + 1)  # Adding 1 to avoid division by zero.\nend  \n\nStep 2: Normalize Driver's Age Using Z-score  \npython\n# Feature name: Normalized_Age_of_Driver  \n# Feature description: Z-score normalization of Age_of_Driver.  \n# Usefulness: Standardizing driver age allows the model to identify relative trends in age distribution without being skewed by extreme values, such as very younger or older drivers, which might correlate with the sex of the driver.\ndf['Normalized_Age_of_Driver'] = (df['Age_of_Driver'] - df['Age_of_Driver'].mean()) / df['Age_of_Driver'].std()\nend  \n\nStep 3: Driver's Age Interaction with Number of Casualties  \npython\n# Feature name: Age_Casualty_Interaction  \n# Feature description: Multiply Age_of_Driver by Number_of_Casualties to capture influence of driver age on accident outcomes.  \n# Usefulness: Drivers of different ages might be more or less likely to be involved in accidents with multiple casualties, revealing behavioral patterns related to the sex of the driver.\ndf['Age_Casualty_Interaction'] = df['Age_of_Driver'] * df['Number_of_Casualties']\nend  \n\n---\n\n### Dropping Columns for Redundancy or Low Predictive Power  \n\nDrop 2nd_Road_Number  \npython\n# Explanation why the column 2nd_Road_Number is dropped  \n# This column is sparse, with most values being 0.0. It does not add meaningful signal or strong correlation towards predicting driver gender.  \ndf.drop(columns=['2nd_Road_Number'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nDrop Pedestrian_Movement  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Pedestrian_Movement is dropped  \n# Values are mostly concentrated around 0, contributing minimal variation or direct relevance to the sex of the driver.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Pedestrian_Movement'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nDrop Vehicle_Location-Restricted_Lane  \npython\n# Explanation why the column Vehicle_Location-Restricted_Lane is dropped  \n# Majority of values are 0 (Q1 = Median = Q3 = 0), indicating low signal importance for predicting driver gender.  \ndf.drop(columns=['Vehicle_Location-Restricted_Lane'], inplace=True)\nend  \n\nThese steps focus on deriving meaningful interactions and normalizations while removing redundant or uninformative columns to enhance downstream model performance."]###,
